.TH SORTOF-MINHASH 1 "October 2026" "sortof-suite" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
sortof\-minhash \- list files in similarity order (MinHash)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sortof\-minhash
[options] SOURCE...
.SH DESCRIPTION
Computes a MinHash digest for every file (128 functions over 8\-byte shingles), orders the files greedily so that files sharing byte sequences end up next to each other, and refines the order with 2\-opt sweeps. The result is a list to pipe into an archiver. The greedy pass starts at the first file, so the order depends on the input order: give it a sorted list, for example sortof\-natural dir | sortof\-minhash \-.
.PP
A source is a directory (scanned recursively), a regular file (listed as it is), or \- for a list of paths on standard input, used once. A path that comes up more than once is listed once.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-b N
Files per batch (default: one batch, which is the most accurate).
.TP
.B \-o N
Most 2\-opt refinement sweeps (default 50; 0 turns them off).
.TP
.B \-m
Matroska\-aware: for real .mkv and .webm files (found by their magic bytes) hash only the Cluster payload, skipping the header, SeekHead, Info, Tracks and Cues. Other files are hashed normally.
.TP
.B \-t N
Threads (default: the number of CPUs).
.TP
.B \-v
Print a memory and behaviour estimate before running.
.TP
.B \-p PATH
Pin PATH: it comes first in the output, exactly as given, and is never sorted, hashed or read. May be repeated. A file named cdcstore.idx is pinned by itself.
.TP
.B \-f PATH
Write the list to PATH instead of standard output.
.TP
.B \-q
Quiet.
.TP
.B \-h
Print help.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
.RS
sortof\-natural dir | sortof\-minhash \- | tar \-T\- \-\-no\-recursion \-cf \- | zstd \-9 > out.tar.zst
.RE
.fi
.sp
